// Generated by scripts/gen-docs.mjs from content/docs/*.md. Don't edit; run `npm run docs:gen`.
export const DOCS: Record<string, string> = {
  "agents.md": "# Rahasya for AI agents\n\nThis page contains everything a coding agent or tool needs to use Rahasya. It is also available as plain Markdown at `https://<your-deployment>/agents.md`.\n\n## Summary\n\n- Rahasya is an **OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions API** for uncensored and abliterated models, served by Venice.\n- Point any OpenAI client at the base URL below and authenticate with an API key. Billing uses prepaid credit; there is no account or card.\n- **A person must create the key and add credit.** Neither action is available through the API.\n- The API exposes two endpoints: `GET /models` and `POST /chat/completions`. It has no embeddings, image or audio endpoints.\n\n## Connection\n\n| | Hosted API | Local proxy (on the user's machine) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Base URL | `https://<your-deployment>/api/v1` | `http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1` |\n| API key | `nsk_…` | The local key printed by the proxy |\n| Prompts pass through | Rahasya's server (never stored), then Venice and the model host | The user's machine, then Venice and the model host |\n\nRead the key from an environment variable such as `RAHASYA_API_KEY`. Do not ask anyone to paste a key into a chat, and never print, log or commit one.\n\nTo confirm that the key works:\n\n```sh\ncurl https://<your-deployment>/api/v1/chat/completions \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $RAHASYA_API_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"model\":\"gemma-4-uncensored\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Say ok\"}],\"max_tokens\":20}'\n```\n\nWith the OpenAI SDK:\n\n```ts\nimport OpenAI from \"openai\";\n\nconst client = new OpenAI({ baseURL: \"https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\", apiKey: process.env.RAHASYA_API_KEY });\nconst reply = await client.chat.completions.create({\n  model: \"large-v2\",\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Hello\" }],\n  max_tokens: 1024,\n});\n```\n\n## Obtaining a key\n\nIf no key is available, ask the person to complete these steps. They require a wallet and at least $5 in any token.\n\n1. Open `https://<your-deployment>` and connect a wallet (EVM, Solana or NEAR).\n2. In the account menu, select **API keys**, then **Sign in with your wallet**.\n3. Select **Add credit** to fund the API wallet.\n4. Select **New key**. For agents, a daily limit, a total limit and a model allowlist are recommended.\n5. Store the key in the environment variable that your tool reads.\n\n## Models\n\n`GET /models` is the authoritative list of models available to a key. Prices are read live from Venice.\n\n| Model id | Tools | Privacy |\n|---|---|---|\n| `large-v2` | yes | Anonymized: a third-party host can read the prompt |\n| `large-flash` | yes | Anonymized |\n| `qwen-3.6-plus` | yes | Anonymized |\n| `deepseek-1017-uncensored` | yes | Private: hosted by Venice, not retained |\n| `deepseek-uncensored` | yes | Private |\n| `qwen-3.8-27b` | yes | Private |\n| `gemma-4-uncensored` | yes | Private (lowest cost with tools) |\n| `venice-uncensored` | yes | Private |\n| `venice-roleplay` | yes | Private |\n| `deepseek-abliterated-tee` | no | TEE with end-to-end encryption |\n| `gemma-4-26b-tee` | no | TEE with end-to-end encryption |\n\n- **Agents that call tools must use a model with tool support.** End-to-end encryption cannot carry tool definitions, so TEE models drop them and receive the conversation as a single encrypted message.\n- **Through the hosted API, TEE prompts are readable on Rahasya's server while in flight,** because that server performs the encryption. For encryption on the user's own device, use the local proxy.\n\n## Request rules (hosted API)\n\nThe proxy applies none of the key limits, parameter filtering or rate limit listed here, and its timeouts differ: a stream stops after 3 minutes of silence or 30 minutes in total, and a non-streamed request to a non-TEE model after 120 seconds (TEE requests always stream upstream, so they get the stream limits). It maps the model id, turns off Venice's system prompt, and enforces the model list and an 8 MB body cap. For TEE models it sends only the text of the messages (the history is folded into the encrypted request; tool definitions, images and every other parameter except the output limit and temperature are dropped), sends no usage chunk in streams, and reports `finish_reason: \"stop\"`. Its errors use the code `proxy_error`, except `insufficient_credit` (402) when the wallet is out of credit.\n\n- A request ends after 3 minutes without data from Venice, or after 30 minutes in total, whether or not it streams. Streaming reports progress sooner. After a timeout or a late failure, the key keeps its reservation, so retry with care.\n- `max_tokens` or `max_completion_tokens` must be between 1 and 32768. **Always set one.** Without it, the key is charged a reservation for the model's maximum output until actual usage is known, which can exhaust a small limit.\n- `n` must be 1. The request body limit is 8 MB, and images are sent base64-encoded.\n- The rate limit is 60 requests per minute per key.\n- Of `venice_parameters`, only `disable_thinking` and `strip_thinking_response` are used. Other keys are removed.\n- In streams, reasoning arrives as `reasoning_content`, and the last chunk before `[DONE]` carries `usage` (except on TEE models).\n\n## Errors (hosted API)\n\nErrors use this shape: `{ \"error\": { \"message\": \"...\", \"type\": \"<code>\", \"code\": \"<code>\" } }`.\n\n| HTTP | `code` | Recommended action |\n|---|---|---|\n| 400 | `bad_json`, `bad_request`, `unknown_model` | Correct the request. Do not retry it unchanged |\n| 401 | `invalid_key`, `expired_key` | Stop and ask the person for a new key |\n| 402 | `insufficient_credit` | Stop and ask the person to add credit |\n| 403 | `model_not_allowed` | Call `GET /models` and choose an allowed model |\n| 413 | `too_large` | Send less content, or fewer images per request |\n| 429 | `daily_limit`, `total_limit` | Stop and inform the person. Retrying will not help |\n| 429 | `rate_limited` | Wait for the next minute, then retry |\n| 503 | `busy` | Too many large requests are in progress. Retry shortly |\n| 5xx | `upstream_error` | Retry with backoff, unless the response includes `x-should-retry: false`, which means the key was charged |\n\n## Local proxy\n\nThe person runs the proxy with Node.js 22 or later:\n\n```sh\nnpx rahasya@0.1.2         # the first run asks for the inference key, with hidden input\nnpx rahasya@0.1.2 token   # prints the local API key\n```\n\nThe inference key can spend the user's credit and move any USDC held by that wallet. Never ask for it or read it. The proxy answers only on localhost and refuses requests that carry a browser `Origin` header, so call it from a CLI, an SDK or an editor rather than a web page.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- [API reference](https://<your-deployment>/docs/api): complete model table, limits and errors\n- [Integrations](https://<your-deployment>/docs/integrations): opencode, Cursor, Continue, Cline, Aider, Zed, SillyTavern, Open WebUI and LiteLLM\n- [How it works](https://<your-deployment>/docs/protocol): wallets, payments, attestation, and what each party can see\n",
  "api.md": "# API reference\n\nThe Rahasya API is compatible with the OpenAI Chat Completions API. Existing OpenAI code works after you change the base URL, the API key and the model id. Usage is paid from prepaid credit, which you can add with any token on almost any chain. No account or card is required.\n\n## Summary\n\n- Base URL `https://<your-deployment>/api/v1`, authenticated with `Authorization: Bearer nsk_…`. Two endpoints: `GET /models` and `POST /chat/completions`.\n- 11 models. Nine support tools; the two TEE models are end-to-end encrypted and do not support tools.\n- Prices are Venice's published prices, read live, with no markup. Credit is prepaid and is added on the website by a person.\n- Each key can have a daily and a total limit in US dollars, a model allowlist and an expiry. The rate limit is fixed at 60 requests per minute per key.\n- Always set `max_tokens` (between 1 and 32768).\n\n```\nBase URL   https://<your-deployment>/api/v1        (local development: http://localhost:3000/api/v1)\nAuth       Authorization: Bearer nsk_…\n```\n\nAny client that implements the OpenAI Chat Completions API is supported, including the official SDKs, opencode, Cursor, Continue, Cline, Aider, SillyTavern, Open WebUI and LiteLLM. Configuration for each is in [Integrations](integrations.md).\n\n## Create an API key\n\n1. Open the site and **connect a wallet** (EVM, Solana or NEAR).\n2. In the account menu, select **API keys**, then **Sign in with your wallet**. This signs a single-use message; it is not a transaction.\n3. Select **Add credit** to fund your API wallet. You can pay from the connected wallet, with any token on any chain through NEAR Intents, or by sending USDC on Base directly. The minimum top-up is $5, which is Venice's minimum.\n4. Select **New key**. Give it a name and, optionally, a daily limit, a total limit, a list of allowed models and an expiry.\n5. **Copy the key immediately.** It is displayed once. Rahasya stores only a hash of it.\n\nKeys consist of `nsk_` followed by 40 URL-safe characters. Treat them as passwords: anyone who holds a key can spend that key's share of your credit, up to its limits.\n\n## Endpoints\n\nThe examples below use two shell variables:\n\n```sh\nexport BASE=https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\nexport KEY=nsk_…\n```\n\n### `GET /v1/models`\n\nReturns the models available to this key: all models, or only those in the key's allowlist.\n\n```sh\ncurl $BASE/models -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\"\n```\n\n```json\n{ \"object\": \"list\", \"data\": [ { \"id\": \"large-v2\", \"object\": \"model\", \"name\": \"Abliterated Large V2\", \"venice_id\": \"abliteration-abliterated-model-large-v2\", \"privacy\": \"anonymized\" } ] }\n```\n\n### `POST /v1/chat/completions`\n\nAccepts a standard OpenAI Chat Completions request and returns a standard response.\n\n```sh\ncurl $BASE/chat/completions \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"model\":\"large-v2\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Hello\"}],\"max_tokens\":200}'\n```\n\n- **`model`**: one of the model ids listed below. The underlying Venice id (`venice_id`) is also accepted.\n- **`stream: true`**: returns Server-Sent Events in the OpenAI chunk format. On every model except the two TEE models, the last chunk before `[DONE]` includes `usage`.\n- **Tools and function calling**: passed through unchanged on every model except the TEE models. TEE models accept only `messages`, `max_tokens` and `temperature`; all other fields, including tools, `top_p`, `response_format`, `venice_parameters` and images, are ignored.\n- **Other parameters** (`temperature`, `max_tokens`, `top_p`, `response_format`, `reasoning_effort` and others) are passed through to Venice. Of `venice_parameters`, only `disable_thinking` and `strip_thinking_response` are used; other keys are removed, because per-request features such as web search cannot be metered per token. Rahasya always sets `include_venice_system_prompt: false`, so the model receives only your prompt.\n- **Output limit**: `max_tokens` or `max_completion_tokens`, between 1 and 32768. If neither is set, the model's maximum applies, and the key is charged a reservation for that maximum until actual usage is known. `n` must be 1.\n- **Timeouts**: a request ends after 3 minutes without data from Venice, or after 30 minutes in total, whether or not it streams. Use `stream: true` for long replies: if a client disconnects from a silent non-streamed request, the key can remain charged the full reservation. When a request times out or fails late, the key keeps its reservation, because Venice may already have generated and billed the reply. A 500, 502 or 503 that occurs quickly is refunded. When the key has been charged, the response includes `x-should-retry: false`.\n\n**Migrating from OpenAI:** change the base URL, the API key and the model id. The request and response formats are unchanged, within the limits described on this page.\n\n### Supported features\n\n| Feature | Support |\n|---|---|\n| Streaming (`stream: true`) | Every model |\n| Tools / function calling | Every model except the two TEE models |\n| Structured output (`response_format`) | Every model except the two TEE models |\n| Reasoning | Depends on the model. Streams deliver it as `reasoning_content`; `venice_parameters.disable_thinking` disables it (not on TEE models) |\n| Image input | Depends on the model; see the model's page on Venice. Images are sent base64-encoded, within the 8 MB limit |\n| `n` other than 1 | Not supported |\n| Embeddings, image generation, audio, the Responses API | Not supported. Only `GET /models` and `POST /chat/completions` are available |\n\n### Models\n\n| Model id | Name | Based on | Privacy | Tools | Fallback price in / out per 1M tokens |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `large-flash` | Abliterated Large Flash | DeepSeek V4.1 Flash | Anonymized (third-party host) | yes | $1.25 / $2.50 |\n| `large-v2` | Abliterated Large V2 | GLM 5.3 | Anonymized (third-party host) | yes | $3 / $5 |\n| `qwen-3.6-plus` | Qwen 3.6 Plus Uncensored | Qwen 3.6 Plus | Anonymized (third-party host) | yes | $0.625 / $3.75 |\n| `deepseek-1017-uncensored` | DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 Uncensored | DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017, abliterated | Private (Venice-hosted) | yes | $0.33 / $1.32 |\n| `deepseek-uncensored` | DeepSeek V4.1 Flash Uncensored | DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, abliterated | Private (Venice-hosted) | yes | $0.33 / $1.32 |\n| `qwen-3.8-27b` | Qwen 3.8 27B Uncensored | Qwen 3.8 27B | Private (Venice-hosted) | yes | $0.45 / $3.20 |\n| `gemma-4-uncensored` | Gemma 4 Uncensored | Gemma 4 | Private (Venice-hosted) | yes | $0.1625 / $0.50 |\n| `venice-uncensored` | Venice Uncensored 1.2 | Mistral Small 24B | Private (Venice-hosted) | yes | $0.20 / $0.90 |\n| `venice-roleplay` | Venice Role Play Uncensored | Mistral Small 24B | Private (Venice-hosted) | yes | $0.50 / $2 |\n| `deepseek-abliterated-tee` | DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 Abliterated | DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017, abliterated | TEE + E2EE (NEAR AI enclave) | no | $0.375 / $1.50 |\n| `gemma-4-26b-tee` | Gemma 4 26B A4B Uncensored | Gemma 4 26B A4B | TEE + E2EE (Phala enclave) | no | $0.19 / $0.88 |\n\nPrices are Venice's and are read live, refreshed every 10 minutes. The table shows the fallback values, which apply only if the live catalog cannot be reached.\n\n**Privacy tiers through the API:**\n\n- **Anonymized:** Venice states that it removes your identity, and a third-party host serves the model. The host can read the prompt.\n- **Private:** Venice hosts the model and states that it does not retain prompts.\n- **TEE:** the model runs inside an attested hardware enclave. Through the API, **our server** is the end-to-end encryption endpoint: it encrypts the request to the enclave and decrypts the reply, so the prompt is in plaintext on our server while the request is in flight. It is never stored. End-to-end encryption cannot carry tool definitions, so tools are dropped for these two models and the conversation history is folded into a single encrypted message. For end-to-end encryption from your own device, use **Truly private** mode on the website or the local proxy described below.\n\n## Limits\n\nEach key can have the following settings:\n\n| Setting | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Daily limit (USD) | Requests are refused once the day's spend (UTC) reaches the limit |\n| Total limit (USD) | Requests are refused once the key's lifetime spend reaches the limit |\n| Allowed models | Requests for other models are refused with `403` |\n| Expiry | The key stops working after this time |\n| Rate | 60 requests per minute per key (fixed) |\n\nSpend is metered per request, from the actual token usage that Venice reports multiplied by the model's live price. Limits are checked **before** each request, so a single long request can finish slightly over a limit; the next request is then refused.\n\nYou can change a key's name, limits and models, or delete the key, at any time. Deletion takes effect immediately.\n\n## Credit and the API wallet\n\nAPI keys spend from your **API wallet**, a Base address whose private key is held by our server and encrypted at rest. The API wallet is used for inference only:\n\n- USDC that arrives on the API wallet is converted into Venice credit through an x402 top-up signed by our server. Conversion happens when the Add credit dialog detects the USDC, or on the next request that finds the credit exhausted; in that case the server tops up and retries the request once. Amounts under $5 remain as USDC, and at most $200 is converted at a time.\n- Venice credit can be spent only on inference. It cannot be withdrawn or transferred, by you or by us. USDC that has not yet been converted is held on a key that our server controls.\n\nWebsite chat uses a separate wallet, the **inference wallet**, which exists only in your browser. The two balances are independent. See [How it works](protocol.md).\n\n## Errors\n\nErrors follow the OpenAI format: `{ \"error\": { \"message\": \"...\", \"type\": \"<code>\", \"code\": \"<code>\" } }`.\n\n| HTTP | `code` | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 400 | `bad_json` | The body is not valid JSON |\n| 400 | `unknown_model` | The model id is not in the list above |\n| 400 | `bad_request` | `max_tokens` is out of range, `n` is not 1, or `messages` is not an array |\n| 400 | other lowercase codes | The request was refused before reaching the model; `message` explains why |\n| 401 | `invalid_key` | The key is wrong, malformed or deleted |\n| 401 | `expired_key` | The key has passed its expiry |\n| 402 | `insufficient_credit` | The API wallet has no credit. Add credit on the site |\n| 403 | `model_not_allowed` | The key's allowlist does not include this model |\n| 413 | `too_large` | The request body exceeds 8 MB. Images are base64-encoded, so send a few at a time |\n| 429 | `daily_limit` / `total_limit` | The key has reached a spend limit |\n| 429 | `rate_limited` | More than 60 requests in one minute on this key |\n| 503 | `busy` | Too many large requests at once (one large request per key at a time, per server instance: a body over 1 MB, or one sent without a declared length). Retry shortly |\n| 4xx / 5xx | `upstream_error` | Venice or the network failed. The status is Venice's when available, otherwise 502. Retry on 5xx |\n\n## Data storage and logging\n\n- **Stored** in Redis, hosted by Upstash: your user id (wallet address); the API wallet (address and encrypted key); each key's **hash**, name, 10-character prefix, settings and last-used time; per-key spend counters; top-up records with their signed x402 authorizations (kept for 7 days); short-lived sign-in challenges and sessions; and per-IP rate-limit counters (kept for 2 minutes).\n- **Hosting logs:** Cloudflare keeps request logs containing metadata such as path, status and IP address, but not request bodies.\n- **Never stored or logged:** prompts, replies and API keys in plaintext.\n- **Visible in transit:** prompts pass through our server on their way to Venice, as described under the privacy tiers above.\n\n## Local proxy alternative\n\nTo keep our server out of the request path, run the proxy on your own machine. It pays from your **inference wallet** directly, and requests to TEE models are then end-to-end encrypted from your device.\n\n```sh\nnpx rahasya@0.1.2    # requires Node.js 22 or later; the first run asks for the key from account menu → Local proxy\n\n# or, as a single readable file:\ncurl -fsSLO https://<your-deployment>/rahasya.mjs\ncurl -fsSLO https://<your-deployment>/rahasya.mjs.sha256\nshasum -a 256 -c rahasya.mjs.sha256   # Linux: sha256sum -c rahasya.mjs.sha256\nnode rahasya.mjs\n```\n\nThe proxy prints `http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1` and a local API key. Later runs start immediately; `node rahasya.mjs --help` lists the other commands. The site publishes the file's SHA-256 checksum at `/rahasya.mjs.sha256` and displays it in the Local proxy dialog. The file is built from `packages/proxy` in this repository (`npm run -w @pay/proxy bundle`), and you can also run the proxy from a clone with `npm run proxy`.\n\nThe model ids are the same. Point your tool at `http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1` with the local key. The proxy applies none of the key limits, parameter filtering or rate limit described above, and its timeouts differ: a stream stops after 3 minutes of silence or 30 minutes in total, and a non-streamed request to a non-TEE model after 120 seconds (TEE requests always stream upstream, so they get the stream limits). It maps the model id, turns off Venice's system prompt, and enforces the model list and an 8 MB body cap. For TEE models it sends only the text of the messages (the history is folded into the encrypted request; tool definitions, images and every other parameter except the output limit and temperature are dropped), sends no usage chunk in streams, and reports `finish_reason: \"stop\"`. Its errors use the code `proxy_error`, except `insufficient_credit` (402) when the wallet is out of credit.\n",
  "index.md": "# Overview\n\nRahasya provides private chat and an OpenAI-compatible API for uncensored and abliterated language models. You pay with the token you already hold, on almost any chain, through NEAR Intents. Rahasya requires no account, email address or card.\n\n## Summary\n\n- **Product.** A chat interface and an OpenAI-compatible API for uncensored and abliterated models, served by Venice. Available at https://<your-deployment>.\n- **Payment.** NEAR Intents (1Click) converts more than 200 tokens on 39 chains into USDC on the user's own Base wallet. A payment can come straight from a shielded pool, such as shielded Zcash, which keeps the payer private; the deposit amount and the USDC delivery on Base stay public. The USDC buys Venice credit over x402, and each request spends that credit with a freshly signed header. No on-chain transaction is made per message.\n- **Truly private mode.** Requests go only to models running in hardware enclaves (TEEs), including one on a NEAR AI enclave, as reported by Venice. The browser verifies the enclave's attestation, including Intel's signature chain, and then encrypts every message to the enclave.\n- **Custody.** Website chat is non-custodial. The wallet that pays for chat is derived in the browser from a single signature and is never sent to our server. It leaves the browser only if you copy it into the local proxy on your own computer.\n- **Developer access.** API keys support daily and total spend limits, a model allowlist and an expiry. A local proxy offers the same API from the user's machine, with encryption performed locally.\n- **Verifiability.** The code is open source (MIT) with 193 offline tests. `npm run verify:tee` checks a live enclave attestation without a wallet or funds.\n\n## Get started\n\n| To… | Start with |\n|---|---|\n| Chat in the browser | [Quickstart: chat](#quickstart-chat) |\n| Call the API from code or a tool | [Quickstart: API](#quickstart-api) |\n| Keep requests on your own machine | [Local proxy](api.md#local-proxy-alternative) |\n| Give a coding agent everything it needs | [For AI agents](agents.md) |\n| Read every page in one file | `https://<your-deployment>/llms-full.txt` |\n\n## Quickstart: chat\n\n1. **Connect a wallet.** Select **Connect wallet** and choose an EVM, Solana or NEAR wallet. The wallet serves as your login.\n2. **Unlock.** Sign one message. Your browser derives a private **inference wallet** from the signature; this wallet pays for your chats. It is never sent to our server, and the same signature recreates it on any device. (The local proxy is the exception you choose: you copy the key into it, and it saves the key in `~/.rahasya` on your computer.)\n3. **Add credit.** Open the account menu and select **Add credit**. You can pay from the connected wallet, send any token from any wallet or exchange (NEAR Intents converts it), or send USDC on Base directly. The minimum is $5.\n4. **Send a message.** Choose a model and a privacy mode in the prompt bar.\n\nCredit typically arrives within one to two minutes. The dialog reports each step as it happens. You can close it at any time; on reload, the site resumes from the last saved step.\n\n## Privacy modes\n\n| Mode | Available models | Who can read the prompt |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Anonymous** | All models | The model host. You are identified only by a wallet address: no email address or card |\n| **Truly private** | The two enclave (TEE) models | Only the attested enclave. The browser verifies the attestation, then encrypts every message to a key held inside the enclave. This assumes the enclave runs the software Venice says it does: its measurement isn't pinned yet ([details](protocol.md#5-truly-private-mode-attestation-e2ee)) |\n\nAfter a Truly private reply, select **Verified enclave** to review each check the browser performed, including Intel's signature chain. For the assumptions behind this mode, see [Truly private mode](protocol.md#5-truly-private-mode-attestation-e2ee).\n\n## Quickstart: API\n\n1. **Create an API key.** In the account menu, select **API keys**, then **Sign in with your wallet**. This signs a message; it is not a transaction. Select **Add credit** to fund your API wallet, then **New key**. You can set a daily limit, a total limit, allowed models and an expiry. The key is displayed once, so copy it before closing the dialog.\n\n2. **Store the key in your environment.**\n\n   ```sh\n   export RAHASYA_API_KEY=nsk_…\n   ```\n\n3. **Send your first request.** The API implements OpenAI Chat Completions, so the official OpenAI SDKs work without changes.\n\n   ```sh\n   curl https://<your-deployment>/api/v1/chat/completions \\\n     -H \"Authorization: Bearer $RAHASYA_API_KEY\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"model\":\"gemma-4-uncensored\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Why is privacy important?\"}],\"max_tokens\":200}'\n   ```\n\n   ```python\n   import os\n   from openai import OpenAI\n\n   client = OpenAI(base_url=\"https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\", api_key=os.environ[\"RAHASYA_API_KEY\"])\n   reply = client.chat.completions.create(\n       model=\"gemma-4-uncensored\",\n       messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Why is privacy important?\"}],\n       max_tokens=200,\n   )\n   print(reply.choices[0].message.content)\n   ```\n\n   ```ts\n   import OpenAI from \"openai\";\n\n   const client = new OpenAI({ baseURL: \"https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\", apiKey: process.env.RAHASYA_API_KEY });\n   const reply = await client.chat.completions.create({\n     model: \"gemma-4-uncensored\",\n     messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Why is privacy important?\" }],\n     max_tokens: 200,\n   });\n   console.log(reply.choices[0].message.content);\n   ```\n\n4. **Connect your tool.** [Integrations](integrations.md) covers opencode, Cursor, Continue, Cline, Aider, Zed, SillyTavern, Open WebUI and LiteLLM. When you create a key, the site also generates a complete opencode configuration with your key and URL filled in.\n\n> **Note:** Always set `max_tokens`. If it is omitted, the key reserves the model's maximum output until the reply completes, which can exhaust a small limit.\n\n## Access methods\n\n| | Website chat | API key | Local proxy |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Runs | On this site | In any tool, on any machine | In any tool, on your machine |\n| Setup | None | Paste a key | Run `npx rahasya@0.1.2`, or download one file |\n| Pays from | Inference wallet (in your browser) | API wallet (signed by our server) | Inference wallet (on your machine) |\n| Per-key spend limits | Not applicable | Daily, total, models, expiry | Not applicable |\n| Prompts pass through our server | No | Yes, in transit only; never stored | No |\n| End-to-end encryption on TEE models | Yes, from your browser | To our server, then to the enclave | Yes, from your machine |\n\nTEE models support chat but not tool calls. Coding agents such as opencode should use a non-TEE model. For private conversations from scripts or chat applications, use a TEE model through the local proxy.\n\n## Next steps\n\n- [API reference](api.md): endpoints, models, prices, limits and errors\n- [Integrations](integrations.md): configuration for each supported tool\n- [How it works](protocol.md): wallets, the payment flow, attestation, and what each party can see\n",
  "integrations.md": "# Integrations\n\nThis page explains how to configure editors, coding agents and chat applications to use Rahasya.\n\n## Summary\n\n- Every tool uses the same base URL and an `nsk_…` API key.\n- Coding agents that call tools require a non-TEE model: `large-v2` (most capable), `large-flash` (fastest) or `deepseek-1017-uncensored` (lowest cost).\n- opencode has been tested end to end, and the site generates its configuration when you create a key. The other configurations follow each tool's published documentation.\n- To keep our server out of the request path, point the same configurations (except Cursor) at the local proxy.\n\n## Endpoint\n\nAll of the tools below connect to the same OpenAI-compatible endpoint:\n\n```\nBase URL   https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\nAPI key    nsk_…   (account menu → API keys)\nModel      large-v2, large-flash, deepseek-1017-uncensored, …   (full list in the API reference)\n```\n\nFor coding agents that call tools (opencode, Cline, Aider, and Continue in agent mode), choose a **non-TEE** model. End-to-end encryption cannot carry tool definitions, so the two TEE models do not support tools.\n\nThe configurations below reflect each tool's documented format as of November 2026. If a tool's settings have moved, look for options labelled \"OpenAI-compatible\", \"custom base URL\" or \"OpenAI API base\".\n\n## opencode\n\nTested with opencode 2.1.3. Add a provider to `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc`, or to `opencode.jsonc` in a project:\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://opencode.ai/config.json\",\n  \"model\": \"rahasya/large-v2\",\n  \"enabled_providers\": [\"rahasya\"],\n  \"providers\": {\n    \"rahasya\": {\n      \"name\": \"Rahasya\",\n      \"env\": [\"RAHASYA_API_KEY\"],\n      \"package\": \"@opencode/ai/providers/openai-compatible\",\n      \"settings\": { \"baseURL\": \"https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\" },\n      \"models\": {\n        \"large-flash\":              { \"name\": \"Abliterated Large Flash\", \"cost\": { \"input\": 1.25, \"output\": 2.5 } },\n        \"large-v2\":                 { \"name\": \"Abliterated Large V2\", \"cost\": { \"input\": 3, \"output\": 5 } },\n        \"qwen-3.6-plus\":            { \"name\": \"Qwen 3.6 Plus Uncensored\", \"cost\": { \"input\": 0.625, \"output\": 3.75 } },\n        \"deepseek-1017-uncensored\": { \"name\": \"DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 Uncensored\", \"cost\": { \"input\": 0.33, \"output\": 1.32 } },\n        \"deepseek-uncensored\":      { \"name\": \"DeepSeek V4.1 Flash Uncensored\", \"cost\": { \"input\": 0.33, \"output\": 1.32 } },\n        \"qwen-3.8-27b\":             { \"name\": \"Qwen 3.8 27B Uncensored\", \"cost\": { \"input\": 0.45, \"output\": 3.2 } },\n        \"gemma-4-uncensored\":       { \"name\": \"Gemma 4 Uncensored\", \"cost\": { \"input\": 0.1625, \"output\": 0.5 } },\n        \"venice-uncensored\":        { \"name\": \"Venice Uncensored 1.2\", \"cost\": { \"input\": 0.2, \"output\": 0.9 } },\n        \"venice-roleplay\":          { \"name\": \"Venice Role Play Uncensored\", \"cost\": { \"input\": 0.5, \"output\": 2 } }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n```sh\nexport RAHASYA_API_KEY=nsk_…\nopencode --standalone                     # or: opencode run --standalone -m rahasya/large-v2 \"explain this repo\"\n```\n\nWhen you create a key, the website shows this configuration with your key and URL filled in. If the key has a model allowlist, the configuration lists only those models.\n\n### Troubleshooting\n\n- **Configuration file name.** opencode reads only `opencode.json` or `opencode.jsonc`. A file named `config.json` is ignored, and opencode falls back to its built-in providers without warning.\n- **\"Invalid API key.\" on the first request.** opencode 2 runs a background service that does not see variables exported after it started. Start opencode with `--standalone` from the terminal where you exported the key.\n- **Other providers appear in the model list.** `enabled_providers` restricts opencode to this provider. Remove that line to keep opencode's built-in providers as well.\n- **Spend shown in the sidebar.** opencode estimates spend from each model's `cost` (US dollars per million tokens, at Venice's list prices). The amount actually charged to your key is shown in the API keys dialog.\n- **Some models are missing.** opencode lists only the models named under `models`. The configuration above includes every model that supports coding agents; the two TEE models are omitted because they do not support tools.\n\n## Cursor\n\nIn Settings, open **Models**:\n\n1. Enable **OpenAI API Key** and paste your `nsk_…` key.\n2. Enable **Override OpenAI Base URL** and enter `https://<your-deployment>/api/v1`.\n3. Select **Add model** for each model id you want, for example `large-v2`, and choose it in chat.\n\nCursor sends requests for custom models from its own servers. Your prompts therefore also pass through Cursor, and Cursor cannot reach a local proxy on `127.0.0.1`. Some features, such as tab completion and certain agent tools, use Cursor's own models regardless of your selection.\n\n## Continue (VS Code and JetBrains)\n\nIn `~/.continue/config.yaml`:\n\n```yaml\nmodels:\n  - name: Abliterated Large V2\n    provider: openai\n    model: large-v2\n    apiBase: https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\n    apiKey: nsk_…\n    roles: [chat, edit, apply]\n```\n\n## Cline, Roo Code and Kilo Code\n\nIn Settings, set **API Provider** to **OpenAI Compatible**:\n\n- Base URL: `https://<your-deployment>/api/v1`\n- API Key: `nsk_…`\n- Model ID: `large-v2`\n\n## Aider\n\n```sh\naider --openai-api-base https://<your-deployment>/api/v1 \\\n      --openai-api-key nsk_… \\\n      --model openai/large-v2\n```\n\nThe `openai/` prefix tells Aider (through LiteLLM) to use the OpenAI-compatible route with your base URL.\n\n## Zed\n\nIn `settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"language_models\": {\n    \"openai_compatible\": {\n      \"Rahasya\": {\n        \"api_url\": \"https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\",\n        \"available_models\": [{ \"name\": \"large-v2\", \"display_name\": \"Abliterated Large V2\", \"max_tokens\": 128000 }]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nZed prompts for the API key the first time you use the provider.\n\n## SillyTavern\n\nIn **API Connections**, select **Chat Completion**, then set **Source** to **Custom (OpenAI-compatible)**:\n\n- Custom Endpoint (Base URL): `https://<your-deployment>/api/v1`\n- Custom API Key: `nsk_…`\n- Model: `venice-roleplay` (tuned for roleplay) or `large-v2`\n\nFor long roleplay sessions, consider a dedicated key with a daily limit.\n\n## Open WebUI\n\nIn **Admin Settings**, open **Connections**, then **OpenAI API**, and add a connection:\n\n- URL: `https://<your-deployment>/api/v1`\n- Key: `nsk_…`\n\nThe models appear in the model picker after you save, because Open WebUI reads `/v1/models`.\n\n## LiteLLM proxy\n\n```yaml\nmodel_list:\n  - model_name: large-v2\n    litellm_params:\n      model: openai/large-v2\n      api_base: https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\n      api_key: os.environ/RAHASYA_API_KEY\n```\n\n## OpenAI SDK\n\nPython:\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\", api_key=os.environ[\"RAHASYA_API_KEY\"])\nreply = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"large-v2\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello\"}],\n    max_tokens=1024,\n)\nprint(reply.choices[0].message.content)\n```\n\nJavaScript or TypeScript, with streaming:\n\n```ts\nimport OpenAI from \"openai\";\n\nconst client = new OpenAI({ baseURL: \"https://<your-deployment>/api/v1\", apiKey: process.env.RAHASYA_API_KEY });\nconst stream = await client.chat.completions.create({\n  model: \"large-flash\",\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Hello\" }],\n  max_tokens: 1024,\n  stream: true,\n});\nfor await (const chunk of stream) process.stdout.write(chunk.choices[0]?.delta?.content ?? \"\");\n```\n\n## curl\n\n```sh\ncurl https://<your-deployment>/api/v1/chat/completions \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $RAHASYA_API_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"model\":\"deepseek-1017-uncensored\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Say hi\"}],\"max_tokens\":50}'\n```\n\n## Spending limits for agents\n\nAn API key can serve as an agent's spending allowance. Give each agent its own key with a **total limit** (for example $2), a **model allowlist** and an **expiry**. When the limit is reached, the API returns `429 total_limit` and the agent cannot start new requests (requests already in flight can finish slightly over the limit), while your other keys and the website continue to work.\n\n## Using the local proxy instead of a hosted key\n\nTo keep requests on your machine, use the same configurations (except Cursor) with the base URL `http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1` and the local key that the proxy prints when you run `npx rahasya@0.1.2`. Setup instructions are in the Local proxy dialog and the [API reference](api.md#local-proxy-alternative).\n",
  "protocol.md": "# How it works\n\nThis page describes how funds move, which wallet pays for what, how each request is authenticated and paid for, and what each party can see. It also identifies which properties you can verify yourself and which depend on a third party. The implementation lives in `packages/rail` (shared logic), `apps/web` (website and API) and `packages/proxy` (local proxy).\n\n## Summary\n\n- **Two wallets.** The inference wallet (website and local proxy) is derived in the browser from a single signature. The website keeps it only in memory and never sends it to our server; the local proxy, if you use it, saves it in `~/.rahasya` after you copy it there. The API wallet (API keys) is held by our server, encrypted, and used for inference credit only.\n- **Funding.** NEAR Intents (1Click) swaps the deposit into USDC on Base, delivers it to one of those wallets, and sends any refund to an address the user controls.\n- **Payment.** x402 adds Venice credit through a gasless USDC authorization. SIWX spends that credit, with a freshly signed header on every request. No on-chain transaction is made per message.\n- **Truly private mode.** A fresh-nonce attestation is verified in the browser, including Intel's signature chain, and each message is then encrypted to the enclave's key with ECDH, HKDF-SHA256 and AES-256-GCM.\n- **Limits of the proof.** The enclave's software measurement is not yet pinned. Against Venice itself, Truly private mode therefore relies on Venice running the enclave software it publishes.\n\n## Components\n\n| Component | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Your wallet** | Any EVM, Solana or NEAR wallet you connect. It serves as your login and the source of funds. Rahasya never receives its key. |\n| **Inference wallet** | A Base address derived in your browser from one signature by your wallet. It pays for website chat and is never sent to our server. To use the local proxy you copy its key to the clipboard and paste it into the proxy, which saves it in `~/.rahasya`; clear the clipboard after pasting. |\n| **API wallet** | A Base address whose key our server holds, encrypted. It pays for requests made with API keys and is used for Venice credit only. |\n| **NEAR Intents (1Click)** | Swaps a deposit in more than 200 tokens on 39 chains into USDC on Base, delivered to one of your wallets. |\n| **x402** | An HTTP payment standard. Your wallet signs a gasless USDC authorization; Venice settles it and credits that wallet. |\n| **SIWX** | Sign-In-With-X. A freshly signed message on every request identifies the paying wallet to Venice, which then charges that wallet's credit. |\n| **Venice** | Runs the models. It provides prepaid credit per wallet, an OpenAI-compatible API and TEE models with end-to-end encryption. |\n\n## 1. The inference wallet (website)\n\nWhen you select **Unlock**, your wallet signs a fixed message (EVM `personal_sign`, Solana `signMessage`, or NEAR NEP-413 `signMessage`). The browser derives a secp256k1 key from the signature:\n\n```\nkey = HKDF-SHA256(ikm = signature, salt = \"pay-anything/inference-wallet/v1\", info = \"<chain>:<lowercased address>\" || 0x00)\n```\n\n- **The same wallet and message always produce the same key,** on any device. Nothing is stored; you unlock again on your next visit. EOA, ed25519 and NEP-413 signatures are deterministic. Smart-contract wallets, whose signatures are not, are detected and refused. EVM signatures are normalized first (v = 27/28, low s), so wallets that encode the same signature differently still produce the same key.\n- **\"The same wallet\" means the same signing key, not the same account name.**\n  - **NEAR:** an account usually has one access key per device or wallet app, and each key produces a different inference wallet. Use the same wallet app to reach your credit. The browser warns you if a different key signs.\n  - **Solana:** a Ledger signs messages in a different format from a software wallet, so the two produce different inference wallets even for the same address.\n- The message contains neither the site's name nor its domain, so renaming or moving the site does not change anyone's wallet. The message itself warns you to sign it only on a site you trust.\n- The key exists only in browser memory.\n\nCode: `packages/rail/src/wallet/derive.ts`, with a golden test vector.\n\n## 2. Funding: any token to USDC on Base (NEAR Intents)\n\n**Add credit** requests a 1Click quote that delivers USDC on Base **to your inference wallet or your API wallet**, with any refund sent **to an address you control**.\n\n- **From your connected wallet:** an `EXACT_OUTPUT` quote for exactly the credit you requested. Your wallet sends the deposit.\n- **Any token, anywhere:** a `FLEX_INPUT` quote. You receive a deposit address (with a QR code and memo when required) and can send from any wallet or exchange. The amount to send is sized about 3% above the credit requested, and whatever arrives is converted and credited, so exchange withdrawal fees do not trigger a refund.\n- **USDC on Base:** send it directly to the wallet; no swap is involved.\n\nEach quote must echo back exactly the parameters requested (assets, amount, recipient and refund address), or it is refused. Progress is tracked through 1Click's status API (`PENDING_DEPOSIT → PROCESSING → SUCCESS | REFUNDED | FAILED`) and saved locally, so a reload resumes the flow instead of starting over.\n\nOur server proxies 1Click only to attach the partner key, our app fee and a referral tag. It holds no funds and permits only quotes that deliver USDC on Base.\n\n**Fees.** On the live site, swaps through NEAR Intents carry a **1% fee**, split evenly between Rahasya and NEAR Intents. The fee is set in server configuration (`ONECLICK_APP_FEE_BPS`; a fresh clone defaults to no fee), and a client cannot change or add one. When paying from your connected wallet, the fee is included in the amount the quote asks you to send, so you receive exactly the credit you requested. With *Any token, anywhere*, the fee is deducted from what you send, and you are credited what arrives. No fee applies to:\n\n- funding with **USDC on Base** directly (no swap);\n- **inference**: you pay Venice's per-token prices without markup, including through API keys.\n\nCode: `apps/web/src/funding/`, `packages/rail/src/oneclick/`.\n\n## 3. Credit: x402 top-up\n\nVenice keeps a **prepaid balance per wallet**. To add to it:\n\n1. `POST /api/v1/x402/top-up` without payment returns `402`, with a `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` header that lists the accepted options (USDC on Base: payee, asset, and a $5 minimum).\n2. The wallet signs an **EIP-3009 `transferWithAuthorization`** for the chosen amount. This is gasless for the wallet; Venice's facilitator submits it.\n3. The request is retried with the `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE` header. Venice settles the payment and credits the wallet.\n\nThe signed authorization is saved **before** it is sent. A retry re-sends the same authorization, whose nonce can settle only once, so a top-up cannot be charged twice.\n\n- **Website:** your browser signs with the inference wallet.\n- **API keys:** our server signs with your API wallet when it converts USDC held there (see section 6).\n\nCode: `packages/rail/src/venice/client.ts`.\n\n## 4. Paying per request: SIWX\n\nEvery request to Venice carries a `SIGN-IN-WITH-X` header: the base64 encoding of a fresh EIP-4361 (Sign-In with Ethereum) message for `api.venice.ai`, with a random nonce and a 5-minute expiry, signed by the paying wallet. Venice charges the request to that wallet's balance. When the balance is exhausted, Venice returns `402` and the wallet tops up again.\n\nx402 therefore **adds** credit, and SIWX **spends** it. No on-chain transaction is made per message.\n\nCode: `packages/rail/src/venice/siwx.ts`.\n\n## 5. Truly private mode: attestation + E2EE\n\nFor the two TEE models, the browser performs the following steps.\n\n1. **Request an attestation.** `GET /tee/attestation?model=…&nonce=<32 random bytes>`. The response includes an Intel TDX quote and NVIDIA GPU evidence. The browser checks:\n   - from the response: that it echoes our nonce, that it is for the requested model, and that `keccak(enclave public key)` equals the signing address;\n   - from the raw quote bytes: that the quote is TDX version 4, that its REPORTDATA contains our nonce (preventing replay) and the enclave's address (binding the key to this hardware), and that the TD debug bit is off.\n\n2. **Verify Intel's signature in the browser.** The quote's ECDSA signature, the quoting enclave's report, Intel's certificate chain up to Intel's root (built into the verifier), the revocation lists and the platform's TCB level are all checked with [`@phala/dcap-qvl`](https://github.com/Phala-Network/dcap-qvl) at a pinned version. The verified report must be the same quote checked in step 1 (same REPORTDATA). The verifier is isolated as follows:\n   - It runs in a disposable Web Worker that first removes storage (IndexedDB, caches, file system) and network access (XHR, WebSocket and others), and replaces `fetch` with a version that can only GET our collateral relay, without cookies. The worker cannot access the page's memory, where your inference key resides.\n   - Intel's public collateral (CRLs, TCB info, QE identity) is fetched through our relay at `/api/tee/pccs/…` and cached for one hour, so your browser does not contact a third party. All collateral is signed by Intel; the relay can withhold it but cannot forge it.\n   - A failed signature check blocks sending. If the collateral cannot be fetched, the browser retries once, then asks you before sending; the other checks still apply.\n\n   The GPU evidence is verified by Venice's verifier and labelled as such.\n\n   **What this proves, and what it does not.** Together, these checks show that the key you encrypt to was generated inside a genuine, non-debug Intel TDX enclave, with a fresh nonce. The website reuses a verified attestation for up to 10 minutes, then requests a new one. The checks do not yet prove *which software* runs in the enclave: its measurement (MRTD) is displayed but not pinned. Truly private mode therefore protects your messages from us, from your network and from the model host's operators; against Venice itself, it relies on Venice running the enclave software it publishes. Pinning measurements is planned. API keys and the local proxy rely on Venice's verifier for the Intel signature; the in-browser check is available on the website only.\n\n3. **Encrypt.** ECDH on secp256k1 with the enclave key, then HKDF-SHA256, then AES-256-GCM, with a fresh ephemeral key for each message. Venice's end-to-end encryption covers only the user and system roles, so the conversation history is folded into a single encrypted message rather than sent as plaintext assistant turns.\n\n4. **Decrypt** the streamed reply. Any chunk that arrives unencrypted is counted, and the interface flags it.\n\nCode: `packages/rail/src/venice/e2ee.ts`, with tests against a captured real attestation in `e2ee.test.ts`. To run the check without keys: `npm run verify:tee`.\n\n## 6. API keys and the API wallet\n\nTools such as opencode cannot sign SIWX messages, so for API keys our server signs on your behalf, using a separate wallet:\n\n```\nopencode ──Bearer nsk_…──▶ /api/v1 (our server)\n                             ├─ key → hash lookup, expiry, model allowlist, spend limits, rate limit\n                             ├─ decrypt your API wallet key (AES-256-GCM, bound to your user id)\n                             ├─ sign a fresh SIWX header ──▶ Venice (charges the API wallet's credit)\n                             └─ read token usage from the reply × live price ──▶ add to the key's spend\n```\n\n- **One API wallet per user.** It is created on first use; its private key is sealed with a server secret and stored in Redis.\n- **Inference only.** USDC sent to the API wallet is converted into Venice credit when the Add credit dialog detects it, or on the next request that finds the credit exhausted (at least $5, and at most $200 at a time). Until then, it remains USDC on a key our server holds. Venice credit cannot be withdrawn, so once converted, value cannot be moved out, even by us. At worst, someone with server access could spend users' API credit on inference.\n- **Keys** are random 240-bit tokens, and only their SHA-256 hashes are stored. Each key has optional daily and total limits in US dollars, a model allowlist, an expiry and a fixed rate limit of 60 requests per minute.\n- **Managing keys** requires a session: your wallet signs a single-use challenge that expires after 5 minutes. EVM signatures are verified with ERC-1271 and ERC-6492 support. NEAR signatures are checked against a full-access key of the account through NEAR RPC.\n\nCode: `apps/web/src/server/` (`keys.ts`, `api-wallet.ts`, `gateway.ts`, `login.ts`), with tests in `server.test.ts`.\n\n## Who can see what\n\n| | Website, Anonymous | Website, Truly private | API key | Local proxy |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Our server** | No prompts (chat goes from the browser to Venice). 1Click quote requests, in which it sees your IP address, inference wallet and refund address together, and adds our partner key, fee and referral | Same | Prompts in transit (not stored), key hashes, spend, 1Click quote requests | Nothing |\n| **Venice** | Your IP address (the browser calls Venice directly), inference wallet address and prompts | IP address, wallet address and **ciphertext**\\* | API wallet address and prompts (TEE models: ciphertext\\*) | Your IP address, inference wallet address and prompts (TEE models: ciphertext\\*) |\n| **Model host** | Prompts (anonymized tier: a third party; private tier: Venice) | Only the attested enclave | Same as Anonymous (TEE models: the enclave) | Same as the website |\n| **1Click solver** | Deposit and delivery addresses and amounts | Same | Same | Nothing |\n| **Public blockchain** | Your deposit to 1Click, the USDC delivered to your wallet, and the x402 settlement | Same | Same | Nothing |\n| **Base RPC provider** | Your IP address and inference wallet address (balance reads about every 30 seconds while unlocked) | Same | Nothing | Nothing |\n| **Reown (wallet modal)** | Your IP address when the page loads its remote configuration; connection metadata when using WalletConnect QR or mobile wallets | Same | Same (sign-in) | Nothing |\n| **Cloudflare, Upstash** | Cloudflare hosts the site and terminates TLS, so it can see what our server sees, and keeps request logs (metadata, not bodies). Upstash hosts the database | Same | Same | Nothing |\n\n\\* The enclave is verified as genuine Intel hardware (in your browser, on the website). Which software runs inside it is not yet pinned, so against Venice itself this relies on Venice running the software it publishes. See \"What this proves, and what it does not\" in section 5.\n\nNo on-chain link exists between your connected wallet and your inference wallet unless you fund one from the other.\n\n## Failure handling\n\n| Situation | Behavior |\n|---|---|\n| The browser closes during a deposit | The saved state resumes. If no transaction hash was returned, you are asked to wait or to send again (1Click refunds any extra deposit). |\n| A deposit arrives short | A `FLEX_INPUT` quote converts it anyway; an `EXACT_OUTPUT` quote is refunded by its deadline. |\n| A swap fails | 1Click refunds to your refund address, and the status shows Refunded. |\n| An x402 top-up is interrupted | The same signed authorization is re-sent; it can settle only once. |\n| The API wallet has no credit | The API returns `402 insufficient_credit`. If USDC is waiting on the API wallet, it is converted and the request is retried once. |\n| A key reaches its limit | The API returns `429`; other keys are unaffected. |\n",
};
